= An Envoy from the North

An envoy of the Empress arrives in Axton — no ultimatum in his case, only courtesies, a wreath for the Founding memorial, and a series of very attentive dinners. The visit is the message: the Empire is taking the Commonwealth's measure, and everyone from the Foreign Ministry to the gutter press understands that the measuring has begun.

[? if empire_posture >= 65: What he finds appears to please him. The Empress's party is ascendant at the northern court, and its language toward the republic has grown almost warm — partnership, patrimony, the healing of old wounds. It is the warmth of a hearth one may yet be invited to sit much too close to. ?][? if empire_posture >= 40 and empire_posture < 65: His courtesies are correct and his questions precise. At the northern court, we are told, the Empress's conciliators and the hardliners still contend for her ear, and nothing in the Commonwealth's condition has yet settled the argument either way. ?][? if empire_posture < 40: The courtesies are thin. At the northern court the hardliners hold the corridor, and their view of the republic requires no embassy to decode: a weak and quarrelsome remnant, holding in trust what the Empire means one day to reclaim. ?]

[? if reunification_support >= 50: What unsettles the Foreign Ministry most is not the envoy but the reception: in the salons, in the propertied press, even in some guild halls, talk of reunion with the north no longer sounds absurd. ?][? if reunification_support < 50: Talk of reunion remains the property of a fringe — royalist salons and a few nostalgic colonels — but the envoy's hosts noted carefully who attended his dinners. ?][? if imperial_fear >= 5: In the streets, the visit lands differently: the north means invasion scares, and the scares are getting easier to start. ?]

The Convention, when it comes, will settle accounts with the north one way or another. The envoy's report will help decide on whose terms.
